                        "content": "     1\u2192---\n     2\u2192title: \"Options\"\n     3\u2192description: \"Options for configuring linkding\"\n     4\u2192---\n     5\u2192\n     6\u2192This document lists the options that linkding can be configured with and explains how to use them in the individual install scenarios.\n     7\u2192\n     8\u2192## Using options\n     9\u2192\n    10\u2192### Docker\n    11\u2192\n    12\u2192Options are passed as environment variables to the Docker container by using the `-e` argument when using `docker run`. For example:\n    13\u2192\n    14\u2192```\n    15\u2192docker run --name linkding -p 9090:9090 -d -e LD_DISABLE_URL_VALIDATION=True sissbruecker/linkding:latest\n    16\u2192```\n    17\u2192\n    18\u2192For multiple options, use one `-e` argument per option.\n    19\u2192\n    20\u2192### Docker-compose\n    21\u2192\n    22\u2192For docker-compose options are configured using an `.env` file. \n    23\u2192Follow the docker-compose setup in the README and copy `.env.sample` to `.env`. Then modify the options in `.env`.\n    24\u2192\n    25\u2192## List of options\n    26\u2192\n    27\u2192### `LD_SUPERUSER_NAME`\n    28\u2192\n    29\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n    30\u2192\n    31\u2192When set, creates an initial superuser with the specified username when starting the container.\n    32\u2192Does nothing if the user already exists.\n    33\u2192\n    34\u2192See [`LD_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD`](#ld_superuser_password) on how to configure the respective password.\n    35\u2192\n    36\u2192### `LD_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD`\n    37\u2192\n    38\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n    39\u2192\n    40\u2192The password for the initial superuser.\n    41\u2192When left undefined, the superuser will be created without a usable password, which means the user can not authenticate using credentials / through the login form, and can only be authenticated using proxy authentication (see [`LD_ENABLE_AUTH_PROXY`](#ld_enable_auth_proxy)).\n    42\u2192\n    43\u2192### `LD_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS`\n    44\u2192\n    45\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n    46\u2192\n    47\u2192Disables background tasks, such as creating snapshots for bookmarks on the [the Internet Archive Wayback Machine](https://archive.org/web/).\n    48\u2192Enabling this flag will prevent the background task processor from starting up, and prevents scheduling tasks.\n    49\u2192This might be useful if you are experiencing performance issues or other problematic behaviour due to background task processing.\n    50\u2192\n    51\u2192### `LD_DISABLE_URL_VALIDATION`\n    52\u2192\n    53\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n    54\u2192\n    55\u2192Completely disables URL validation for bookmarks.\n    56\u2192This can be useful if you intend to store non fully qualified domain name URLs, such as network paths, or you want to store URLs that use another protocol than `http` or `https`.\n    57\u2192\n    58\u2192### `LD_REQUEST_TIMEOUT`\n    59\u2192\n    60\u2192Values: `Integer` as seconds | Default = `60`\n    61\u2192\n    62\u2192Configures the request timeout in the uwsgi application server. This can be useful if you want to import a bookmark file with a high number of bookmarks and run into request timeouts.\n    63\u2192\n    64\u2192### `LD_SERVER_HOST`\n    65\u2192\n    66\u2192Values: Valid address for socket to bind to | Default = `[::]`\n    67\u2192\n    68\u2192Allows to set a custom host for the UWSGI server running in the container. The default creates a dual stack socket, which will respond to IPv4 and IPv6 requests. IPv4 requests are logged as IPv4-mapped IPv6 addresses, such as \"::ffff:127.0.0.1\". If reverting to an IPv4-only socket is desired, this can be set to \"0.0.0.0\".\n    69\u2192\n    70\u2192### `LD_SERVER_PORT`\n    71\u2192\n    72\u2192Values: Valid port number | Default = `9090`\n    73\u2192\n    74\u2192Allows to set a custom port for the UWSGI server running in the container. While Docker containers have their own IP address namespace and port collisions are impossible to achieve, there are other container solutions that share one. Podman, for example, runs all containers in a pod under one namespace, which results in every port only being allowed to be assigned once. This option allows to set a custom port in order to avoid collisions with other containers.\n    75\u2192\n    76\u2192### `LD_CONTEXT_PATH`\n    77\u2192\n    78\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n    79\u2192\n    80\u2192Allows configuring the context path of the website. Useful for setting up Nginx reverse proxy.\n    81\u2192The context path must end with a slash. For example: `linkding/`\n    82\u2192\n    83\u2192### `LD_ENABLE_AUTH_PROXY`\n    84\u2192\n    85\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n    86\u2192\n    87\u2192Enables support for authentication proxies such as Authelia.\n    88\u2192This effectively disables credentials-based authentication and instead authenticates users if a specific request header contains a known username.\n    89\u2192You must make sure that your proxy (nginx, Traefik, Caddy, ...) forwards this header from your auth proxy to linkding. Check the documentation of your auth proxy and your reverse proxy on how to correctly set this up.\n    90\u2192\n    91\u2192Note that this automatically creates new users in the database if they do not already exist.\n    92\u2192\n    93\u2192Enabling this setting also requires configuring the following options:\n    94\u2192- `LD_AUTH_PROXY_USERNAME_HEADER` - The name of the request header that the auth proxy passes to the proxied application (linkding in this case), so that the application can identify the user.\n    95\u2192Check the documentation of your auth proxy to get this information.\n    96\u2192Note that the request headers are rewritten in linkding: all HTTP headers are prefixed with `HTTP_`, all letters are in uppercase, and dashes are replaced with underscores.\n    97\u2192For example, for Authelia, which passes the `Remote-User` HTTP header, the `LD_AUTH_PROXY_USERNAME_HEADER` needs to be configured as `HTTP_REMOTE_USER`.\n    98\u2192- `LD_AUTH_PROXY_LOGOUT_URL` - The URL that linkding should redirect to after a logout.\n    99\u2192By default, the logout redirects to the login URL, which means the user will be automatically authenticated again.\n   100\u2192Instead, you might want to configure the logout URL of the auth proxy here.\n   101\u2192\n   102\u2192### `LD_ENABLE_OIDC`\n   103\u2192\n   104\u2192Values: `True`, `False` | Default = `False`\n   105\u2192\n   106\u2192Enables support for OpenID Connect (OIDC) authentication, allowing to use single sign-on (SSO) with OIDC providers.\n   107\u2192When enabled, this shows a button on the login page that allows users to authenticate using an OIDC provider.\n   108\u2192Users are associated by the email address provided from the OIDC provider, which is used as the username in linkding.\n   109\u2192If there is no user with that email address as username, a new user is created automatically. \n   110\u2192\n   111\u2192This requires configuring a number of options, which of those you need depends on which OIDC provider you use and how it is configured.\n   112\u2192In general, you should find the required information in the UI of your OIDC provider, or its documentation. \n   113\u2192\n   114\u2192The options are adopted from the [mozilla-django-oidc](https://mozilla-django-oidc.readthedocs.io/en/stable/) library, which is used by linkding for OIDC support.\n   115\u2192Please check their documentation for more information on the options.\n   116\u2192\n   117\u2192The following options can be configured:\n   118\u2192- `OIDC_OP_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT` - The authorization endpoint of the OIDC provider.\n   119\u2192- `OIDC_OP_TOKEN_ENDPOINT` - The token endpoint of the OIDC provider.\n   120\u2192- `OIDC_OP_USER_ENDPOINT` - The user info endpoint of the OIDC provider.\n   121\u2192- `OIDC_OP_JWKS_ENDPOINT` - The JWKS endpoint of the OIDC provider.\n   122\u2192- `OIDC_RP_CLIENT_ID` - The client ID of the application.\n   123\u2192- `OIDC_RP_CLIENT_SECRET` - The client secret of the application.\n   124\u2192- `OIDC_RP_SIGN_ALGO` - The algorithm the OIDC provider uses to sign ID tokens. Default is `RS256`.\n   125\u2192- `OIDC_USE_PKCE` - Whether to use PKCE for the OIDC flow. Default is `True`.\n   126\u2192- `OIDC_VERIFY_SSL` - Whether to verify the SSL certificate of the OIDC provider. Set to `False` if using self-signed certificates or custom certificate authority. Default is `True`.\n   127\u2192\n   128\u2192<details>\n   129\u2192\n   130\u2192<summary>Authelia Example</summary>\n   131\u2192\n   132\u2192#### Linkding Configuration\n   133\u2192\n   134\u2192```bash\n   135\u2192LD_ENABLE_OIDC=True\n   136\u2192OIDC_OP_AUTHORIZATION_ENDPOINT=https://auth.example.com/api/oidc/authorization\n   137\u2192OIDC_OP_TOKEN_ENDPOINT=https://auth.example.com/api/oidc/token\n   138\u2192OIDC_OP_USER_ENDPOINT=https://auth.example.com/api/oidc/userinfo\n   139\u2192OIDC_OP_JWKS_ENDPOINT=https://auth.example.com/jwks.json\n   140\u2192OIDC_RP_CLIENT_ID=linkding\n   141\u2192OIDC_RP_CLIENT_SECRET=myClientSecret\n   142\u2192```\n   143\u2192#### Authelia Configuration\n   144\u2192\n   145\u2192```yaml\n   146\u2192identity_providers:\n   147\u2192  oidc:\n   148\u2192    # --- more OIDC provider configuration ---\n   149\u2192\n   150\u2192    clients:\n   151\u2192      - id: linkding\n   152\u2192        description: Linkding\n   153\u2192        # docker run --rm authelia/authelia:latest authelia crypto rand --length 64 --charset alphanumeric\n   154\u2192        secret: myClientSecret\n   155\u2192        public: false\n   156\u2192        token_endpoint_auth_method: client_secret_post\n   157\u2192        scopes:\n   158\u2192          - openid\n   159\u2192          - email\n   160\u2192          - profile\n   161\u2192        redirect_uris:\n   162\u2192          - https://linkding.example.com/oidc/callback/\n   163\u2192```\n   164\u2192\n   165\u2192</details>\n   166\u2192\n   167\u2192### `LD_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`\n   168\u2192\n   169\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n   170\u2192\n   171\u2192List of trusted origins / host names to allow for `POST` requests, for example when logging in, or saving bookmarks.\n   172\u2192For these type of requests, the `Origin` header must match the `Host` header, otherwise the request will fail with a `403` status code, and the message `CSRF verification failed.`\n   173\u2192\n   174\u2192This option allows to declare a list of trusted origins that will be accepted even if the headers do not match. This can be the case when using a reverse proxy that rewrites the `Host` header, such as Nginx.\n   175\u2192\n   176\u2192For example, to allow requests to https://linkding.mydomain.com, configure the setting to `https://linkding.mydomain.com`.\n   177\u2192Note that the setting **must** include the correct protocol (`https` or `http`), and **must not** include the application / context path.\n   178\u2192Multiple origins can be specified by separating them with a comma (`,`).\n   179\u2192\n   180\u2192This setting is adopted from the Django framework used by linkding, more information on the setting is available in the [Django documentation](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/4.0/ref/settings/#std-setting-CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS).\n   181\u2192\n   182\u2192### `LD_LOG_X_FORWARDED_FOR`\n   183\u2192\n   184\u2192Values: `true` or `false` | Default =  `false`\n   185\u2192\n   186\u2192Set uWSGI [log-x-forwarded-for](https://uwsgi-docs.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Options.html?#log-x-forwarded-for) parameter allowing to keep the real IP of clients in logs when using a reverse proxy.\n   187\u2192\n   188\u2192### `LD_DB_ENGINE`\n   189\u2192\n   190\u2192Values: `postgres` or `sqlite` | Default = `sqlite`\n   191\u2192\n   192\u2192Database engine used by linkding to store data.\n   193\u2192Currently, linkding supports SQLite and PostgreSQL.\n   194\u2192By default, linkding uses SQLite, for which you don't need to configure anything.\n   195\u2192All the other database variables below are only required for configured PostgresSQL.\n   196\u2192\n   197\u2192### `LD_DB_DATABASE`\n   198\u2192\n   199\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `linkding`\n   200\u2192\n   201\u2192The name of the database. \n   202\u2192\n   203### `LD_DB_USER`\n   204\u2192\n   205\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `linkding`\n   206\u2192\n   207\u2192The name of the user to connect to the database server.\n   208\u2192\n   209\u2192### `LD_DB_PASSWORD`\n   210\u2192\n   211\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  None\n   212\u2192\n   213\u2192The password of the user to connect to the database server.\n   214\u2192The password must be configured when using a database other than SQLite, there is no default value.\n   215\u2192\n   216\u2192### `LD_DB_HOST`\n   217\u2192\n   218\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `localhost`\n   219\u2192\n   220\u2192The hostname or IP of the database server.\n   221\u2192\n   222\u2192### `LD_DB_PORT`\n   223\u2192\n   224\u2192Values: `Integer` | Default =  None\n   225\u2192\n   226\u2192The port of the database server.\n   227\u2192Should use the default port if left empty, for example `5432` for PostgresSQL.\n   228\u2192\n   229\u2192### `LD_DB_OPTIONS`\n   230\u2192\n   231\u2192Values: `String` | Default = `{}`\n   232\u2192\n   233\u2192A json string with additional options for the database. Passed directly to OPTIONS.\n   234\u2192\n   235\u2192### `LD_FAVICON_PROVIDER`\n   236\u2192\n   237\u2192Values: `String` | Default =  `https://t1.gstatic.com/faviconV2?client=SOCIAL&type=FAVICON&fallback_opts=TYPE,SIZE,URL&url={url}&size=32`\n   238\u2192\n   239\u2192The favicon provider used for downloading icons if they are enabled in the user profile settings.\n   240\u2192The default provider is a Google service that automatically detects the correct favicon for a website, and provides icons in consistent image format (PNG) and in a consistent image size.\n   241\u2192\n   242\u2192This setting allows to configure a custom provider in form of a URL.\n   243\u2192When calling the provider with the URL of a website, it must return the image data for the favicon of that website.\n   244\u2192The configured favicon provider URL must contain a placeholder that will be replaced with the URL of the website for which to download the favicon.\n   245\u2192The available placeholders are:\n   246\u2192- `{url}` - Includes the scheme and hostname of the website, for example `https://example.com`\n   247\u2192- `{domain}` - Includes only the hostname of the website, for example `example.com`\n   248\u2192\n   249\u2192Which placeholder you need to use depends on the respective favicon provider, please check their documentation or usage examples.\n   250\u2192See the default URL for how to insert the placeholder to the favicon provider URL.\n   251\u2192\n   252\u2192Alternative favicon providers:\n   253\u2192- DuckDuckGo: `https://icons.duckduckgo.com/ip3/{domain}.ico`\n   254\u2192\n   255\u2192\n   256\u2192### `LD_SINGLEFILE_TIMEOUT_SEC`\n   257\u2192\n   258\u2192Values: `Float` | Default =  60.0\n   259\u2192\n   260\u2192When creating HTML archive snapshots, control the timeout for how long to wait for the snapshot to complete, in `seconds`.\n   261\u2192Defaults to 60 seconds; on lower-powered hardware you may need to increase this value. \n   262\u2192\n   263\u2192### `LD_SINGLEFILE_OPTIONS`\n   264\u2192\n   265\u2192Values: `String` | Default = None\n   266\u2192\n   267\u2192When creating HTML archive snapshots, pass additional options to the `single-file` application that is used to create snapshots.\n   268\u2192See `single-file --help` for complete list of arguments, or browse source: https://github.com/gildas-lormeau/single-file-c